      {
        "_uid": "8f81f6c4-7aa3-939a-4edb-972bb741154e",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Dr Mehnaz added, \"Infants who are not blue may have a disease that causes recurrent pneumonia or congestive cardiac failure. Such babies can be managed on drug therapy but require repeated cardiovascular examination for timely cardiac surgery.\" ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Dr Mehnaz concluded by saying that there are other cardiac diseases which an infant or a child can acquire later in life, most importantly, the rheumatic heart disease. This disease afflicts children above five years of age and stems from an initial sore throat caused by a specific bacterial agent called \"Group A, B-hemolytic streptococcus\". Rheumatic fever manifests most of the time with either joint pains or cardiac disease. Patients with this disease require long term preventive treatment with Penicillin.\"",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
